## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses your ability to design a memory-bounded streaming algorithm for heavy hitters with a fixed-coverage threshold.

## Key Concepts
- **Heavy Hitters:** Values that appear frequently in the stream
- **Streaming Algorithms:** Process data incrementally without storing the entire dataset
- **Bounded Memory:** Fixed-size data structures regardless of stream length
- **Threshold-Based Reporting:** Selecting the minimum subset that meets a coverage target

## Algorithm Analysis
- **Per-Element Update Cost:** O(log k) for heap maintenance (hash map lookup is O(1) average)
- **Per-Batch Query Cost:** O(k log k) to sort the heap and accumulate to the 95% threshold
- **Memory Usage:** O(M) for counts (if M is small) or O(unique values) with a hash map, plus O(k) for the heap

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinHeap {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.heap = [];
    this.capacity = capacity;
  }
  push(item) {
    this.heap.push(item);
    this.bubbleUp(this.heap.length - 1);
    if (this.heap.length > this.capacity) this.pop();
  }
  pop() {
    const min = this.heap[0];
    const end = this.heap.pop();
    if (this.heap.length > 0) {
      this.heap[0] = end;
      this.bubbleDown(0);
    }
    return min;
  }
  bubbleUp(i) {
    while (i > 0) {
      const p = Math.floor((i - 1) / 2);
      if (this.heap[p].count <= this.heap[i].count) break;
      [this.heap[p], this.heap[i]] = [this.heap[i], this.heap[p]];
      i = p;
    }
  }
  bubbleDown(i) {
    while (true) {
      const l = 2 * i + 1, r = 2 * i + 2;
      let smallest = i;
      if (l < this.heap.length && this.heap[l].count < this.heap[smallest].count) smallest = l;
      if (r < this.heap.length && this.heap[r].count < this.heap[smallest].count) smallest = r;
      if (smallest === i) break;
      [this.heap[i], this.heap[smallest]] = [this.heap[smallest], this.heap[i]];
      i = smallest;
    }
  }
}

const counts = new Map();
let total = 0;
const k = 20;
const heap = new MinHeap(k);

function updateBatch(batch) {
  for (const v of batch) {
    const count = (counts.get(v) || 0) + 1;
    counts.set(v, count);
    total++;
    heap.push({ value: v, count });
  }
}

function get95Cover() {
  const sorted = [...heap.heap].sort((a, b) => b.count - a.count);
  const threshold = total * 0.95;
  let sum = 0;
  const result = [];
  for (const item of sorted) {
    sum += item.count;
    result.push(item.value);
    if (sum >= threshold) break;
  }
  if (sum < threshold) {
    return [...counts.keys()];
  }
  return result;
}
```

## Trade-offs
The heap-only approach tracks only the top-k values, so if fewer than k values dominate the stream, we may not reach 95% coverage. In such cases, the algorithm falls back to returning all seen values. For guaranteed bounded memory with approximate coverage, consider probabilistic methods like Count-Min Sketch or Lossy Counting.

## Why This Is Asked
Explores practical dynamic approximate all-pairs shortest paths (APSP) with shared structure; tests understanding of trade-offs between update costs and query time in batch-dynamic settings.

## Key Concepts
- Batch-dynamic graphs with nonnegative edge weights
- Hub-based distance sketches for approximate APSP
- Amortized analysis across multiple query pairs
- Trade-offs between approximation accuracy ε and computational efficiency
- Incremental shortest path algorithms

## Code Example
```javascript
// Hub-based distance approximation: dist(u,v) ≈ min_h∈H [dist(u,h) + dist(h,v)]

class HubBasedAPSP {
  constructor(graph, epsilon) {
    this.k = Math.ceil((1/epsilon) * Math.log(graph.n));
    this.hubs = this.selectHubs(graph);
    this.distances = new Map(); // hub -> vertex distances
  }

  updateBatch(edges) {
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      this.distances.set(hub, this.incrementalDijkstra(hub, edges));
    }
  }

  query(u, v) {
    let minDist = Infinity;
    for (const hub of this.hubs) {
      const dist = this.distances.get(hub).get(u) + 
                   this.distances.get(hub).get(v);
      minDist = Math.min(minDist, dist);
    }
    return minDist;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates your ability to design efficient dynamic ranking systems with batched updates, requiring you to balance update locality against query efficiency in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Ordered maps for score-based organization
- Lazy maximum score tracking to avoid unnecessary recomputation
- Efficient top-K extraction through bucket traversal
- Amortized time complexity analysis
- Memory usage optimization techniques

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode implementation
class TopKKeeper {
  constructor(users) {
    this.scoreToIds = new TreeMap(); // score -> TreeSet of user IDs
    this.userToScore = new HashMap(); // user ID -> current score
    this.maxScore = null;

    // Initialize in O(n)
    for (const [id, score] of users) {
      this.addUserToBucket(id, score);
    }
  }

  updateBatch(updates) {
    // Process B updates in O(B log n)
    for (const [id, delta] of updates) {
      const oldScore = this.userToScore.get(id);
      const newScore = oldScore + delta;

      this.removeUserFromBucket(id, oldScore);
      this.addUserToBucket(id, newScore);
    }
  }

  getTopK(k) {
    // Extract top-K in O(K log n)
    const result = [];
    let currentScore = this.maxScore;
    let remaining = k;

    while (remaining > 0 && currentScore !== null) {
      const ids = this.scoreToIds.get(currentScore);
      const takeCount = Math.min(remaining, ids.size());

      // Extract first 'takeCount' IDs (already sorted)
      result.push(...Array.from(ids).slice(0, takeCount));
      remaining -= takeCount;
      currentScore = this.scoreToIds.lowerKey(currentScore);
    }

    return result.slice(0, k);
  }
}
```

## Optimizations
1. **Lazy Maximum Updates**: Only recompute the maximum score when the current maximum bucket becomes empty, avoiding O(log n) overhead on every update.

2. **Batch Top-K Extraction**: Cache the current top-K result and only recompute when updates affect users in the top-K, reducing query frequency from every batch to only when necessary.

## Comparison to Rebuilding
Rebuilding from scratch would require O(n) time per batch to sort all users, resulting in O(n × batches) total time. Our approach achieves O(B log n) updates and O(K log n) queries, providing significant improvement when K << n and B is moderate.

## Complexity Analysis
- **Initialization**: O(n) - one insertion per user
- **Batch Updates**: O(B log n) - each update requires remove/insert operations
- **Top-K Query**: O(K log n) - traverse at most K score buckets
- **Space**: O(n) - store user-score mappings and score buckets

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic skyline maintenance with batch updates, including amortized analysis and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- **2D dominance and Pareto frontier**: A point p dominates q if p.x ≥ q.x and p.y ≥ q.y with at least one strict inequality.
- **Invariant**: Frontier F is sorted by x with strictly increasing y, ensuring no two frontier points dominate each other.
- **Amortized analysis**: Each point is inserted once and removed at most once over n total points.
- **Space**: Uses O(n) space for all historical points plus O(|F|) for the current frontier.

## Data Structure
Use a balanced binary search tree (e.g., TreeMap) keyed by x-coordinate, with y-values stored alongside. This enables O(log |F|) insertion, O(1) access to the rightmost (largest x) point, and O(|F|) in-order traversal.

## Algorithm
1. Sort incoming batch by x-coordinate (O(B log B)).
2. Merge with frontier: for each batch point p, if p.y > current max frontier y, insert p and prune any frontier points with y ≤ p.y.
3. Enumerate frontier via in-order traversal.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Skyline {
  constructor() { this.frontier = new Map(); }

  insertBatch(points) {
    const sorted = points.sort((a, b) => a.x - b.x);
    let maxY = 0;

    for (const p of sorted) {
      if (p.y <= maxY) continue;

      for (const [x, y] of this.frontier) {
        if (y <= p.y) this.frontier.delete(x);
      }

      this.frontier.set(p.x, p.y);
      maxY = p.y;
    }
  }

  getFrontier() {
    return Array.from(this.frontier.entries())
      .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Summary
| Operation | Cost |
|-----------|------|
| Batch insertion | O(B log B + |F|) amortized |
| Enumerate frontier | O(|F|) |
| Space | O(n) |

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design streaming window data structures and perform amortized analysis under batched inputs, a common real-world challenge.

## Key Concepts

